  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem is non-convex due to the quadratic overlap integral. A direct convex optimization approach isn't feasible. Instead, we'll use a constrained local optimization approach with scipy.optimize, leveraging the initial feasible solution and focusing on reducing the maximum overlap term step by step.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points), sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k (sum_{i} h[i]*(1 - h[i +k}) * dx)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Use scipy.optimize with method 'SLSQP' to handle constraints and non-convexity\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize:\n   - If initial_h_values is provided and feasible, use it.\n   - Else, construct a feasible h with sum(h) = n_points/2 and h \u2208 [0,1]\n\nb. Optimization:\n   - Define objective function computing maximum overlap\n   - Set up constraints for h \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = n_points/2\n   - Use scipy.optimize.minimize with method='SLSQP'\n   - Track best solution and terminate on budget or convergence\n\nc. Postprocessing:\n   - Validate final h satisfies constraints\n   - Recompute C\u2085 to ensure accuracy\n   - Return best h, its C\u2085, and n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Default n_points=100 for balance between resolution and speed\n- Start with initial_h_values or random feasible\n- Use gradient-based SLSQP for local optimization\n- Multiple initialization attempts if time allows\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- scipy.optimize.run with time limit handling\n- Check feasibility after each iteration\n- Catch exceptions and return best solution found\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u22641 and sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Recompute C\u2085 using evaluator\n- Return h, C\u2085, and n_points",
